fetchItem, url: https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/22611-epinephrine-adrenaline mergedData: {
  link: 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/22611-epinephrine-adrenaline',
  name: 'adrenaline',
  description: 'A medical information article explaining epinephrine (adrenaline), its biological functions, and its use as a life-saving medication.',
  introduction: '# Epinephrine (Adrenaline): Function, Role, and Medical Use\n' +
    '\n' +
    'This article from the Cleveland Clinic Health Library provides a comprehensive overview of **epinephrine**, commonly known as **adrenaline**. It is a critical biochemical compound that acts as both a neurotransmitter and a hormone within the human body.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '## Key Biological Functions\n' +
    '\n' +
    '*   **Fight-or-Flight Response:** Epinephrine is the primary hormone released by the adrenal glands during moments of acute stress or danger. This triggers a cascade of physiological changes, including increased heart rate, elevated blood pressure, dilated pupils, and the redirection of blood flow to essential muscles.\n' +
    '*   **Neurotransmitter Role:** In the central nervous system, it helps transmit nerve signals, playing a role in metabolism, attention, focus, panic, and excitement.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '## Medical Applications\n' +
    '\n' +
    'Synthetic epinephrine is a vital medication used to treat several life-threatening conditions:\n' +
    '\n' +
    '*   **Anaphylaxis:** First-response treatment for severe allergic reactions.\n' +
    '*   **Severe Asthma:** Helps open airways and reduce spasms.\n' +
    '*   **Cardiac Arrest:** Used during CPR to stimulate the heart.\n' +
    '*   **Septic Shock & Low Blood Pressure:** Helps increase blood pressure in critical situations.\n' +
    '*   **Eye Surgery:** Maintains pupil dilation.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '## Health Implications of Imbalance\n' +
    '\n' +
    'The article details the health conditions that can result from abnormal levels:\n' +
    '\n' +
    '*   **Low Levels:** May be associated with anxiety, depression, headaches, sleep problems, and low blood sugar.\n' +
    '*   **High Levels:** Can lead to hypertension, rapid heartbeat, excessive sweating, severe headaches, and jitters. A specific adrenal gland tumor called a **pheochromocytoma** is also a known cause of high levels.\n' +
    '\n' +
    'The resource includes a comparison table contrasting epinephrine with the related compound **norepinephrine (noradrenaline)**, highlighting their different primary functions and origins.',
  categories: [ 'Search and Research' ],
  tags: [ 'Tech', 'Tutorial' ],
  image: 'https://assets.clevelandclinic.org/transform/LargeFeatureImage/aa41aba3-3189-4d99-9f49-b70fe103eef2/',
  icon: 'https://icons.duckduckgo.com/ip3/my.clevelandclinic.org.ico'
}
